<P>The <a href=https://veramine.com/#team>Veramine engineering team</a> has been hard at work developing our new security monitoring, detection, and response platform.  Our product is deployed now at various corporations around the world and we are <i><u>delighted</u></i> each time a customer is able to use it to detect and repel a determined adversary out of their network.  We are starting this new blog to begin talking more about our technology and how it can be used to detect and respond to APT-style threats.  We thought it might be interesting to start with a few interesting notes about the NSA and CIA hacking tools and techniques.</p>

<P><B>User-mode code injection and persistence</b></p>

<p>An attacker has many choices to inject code and persist in user-mode.  Microsoft provides the <a href=https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/ms682437(v=vs.85).aspx>CreateRemoteThread API</a> to facilitate code injection.  Clever security researchers have also discovered numerous undocumented and stealthy techniques, such as <a href=https://breakingmalware.com/injection-techniques/code-less-code-injections-and-0-day-techniques/>https://breakingmalware.com/injection-techniques/code-less-code-injections-and-0-day-techniques/</a> and <a href=https://github.com/ionescu007/HookingNirvana>https://github.com/ionescu007/HookingNirvana</a>.  The simplest persistence mechanism is to simply add an entry to the <a href=https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/aa376977(v=vs.85).aspx>“Run” registry key</a> to have an arbitrary command executed during Windows logon.  One can also add a DLL to the <a href=https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/aa376977(v=vs.85).aspx>“AppInit_DLLs” list</a> to have it injected into every process.  Yet another method is to use the <a href=https://www.blackhat.com/docs/eu-15/materials/eu-15-Pierce-Defending-Against-Malicious-Application-Compatibility-Shims-wp.pdf>ShimEngine infrastructure</a> to inject a DLL into every process.  These are only a small list of possibilities.</P>

<p>The Vault7 Wikileaks leaks revealed that the CIA is both practical and creative when it comes to user-mode code injection and persistence.  They used several practical code injection and persistence methods including DLL hijacking (<a href=https://wikileaks.org/ciav7p1/cms/page_26968090.html>https://wikileaks.org/ciav7p1/cms/page_26968090.html</a>), Run key and service DLLs (<a href=https://wikileaks.org/ciav7p1/cms/page_51478543.html>https://wikileaks.org/ciav7p1/cms/page_51478543.htm</a>).  DLL hijacking is simply replacing a legitimate DLL that an application is known to load with a malicious one, or placing a malicious DLL in such a way that it is found before the legitimate DLL in the search order.  One instance in the Vault7 leak was replacing “scilexer.dll” with a trojaned version in such a way that code would be injected into the “Notepad++” process.  This evidentally evaded detection on the CIA's targets because Notepad++ normally loaded a DLL named scilexer.dll.  The CIA is not the only one using this technique - it is also used by the PlugX malware family (<a href=http://blog.trendmicro.com/trendlabs-security-intelligence/new-wave-of-plugx-targets-legitimate-apps/>http://blog.trendmicro.com/trendlabs-security-intelligence/new-wave-of-plugx-targets-legitimate-apps/</a>).  Ironically both are associated with nation state malware from opposing continents.  The Windows Service DLL persistence mechanism was another way the CIA bypassed detection.  Windows services can be hosted inside the service hosting process (svchost.exe); to do this, you simply create a DLL with the appropriate entry points and register it with the service control manager and it will be loaded.  This is how native Windows services are developed.</P>

<p>There is no technical eliteness in these techniques.  In fact, some security engineers would laugh at their lack of novelty.  However, if they are so “lame”, why didn’t anyone find the CIA malware years ago?  We will come back to this question later.</p>

<p>The creative technique that caught our attention was Grasshopper "Icon Overlay" module used for persistence (https://wikileaks.org/ciav7p1/cms/page_51478543.html).  Apparently icon overlays are actually COM DLLs loaded by the explorer.exe shell.  This method is publicly documented on MSDN but we had never heard of it until the leak.</P>

<p>At Veramine, we do not think these techniques are “lame” in any way.  They are highly effective because they are used in accordance with the “hiding in plain sight” principle.  They blend in with normal application behavior and consequently make it difficult for both security software and human analysts to identify malicious intention.  One might ask, why not trigger on applications loading DLLs from the current directory or “Downloads” folder (or any other place that may seen out of the ordinary)?  If there were a standard on how DLLs should be loaded then that idea might have some merit.  In real life, application developers can do whatever they want with their code; if this rule were to be applied, the analyst would be overloaded with spurious alerts.  For example, we have seen security software injecting a DLL into every process and mapping random executables into the host process; from Windows’ perspective, this will look like the process is loading thousands of DLLs.  As for the service DLLs, they blend in because all hosted services are loaded the exact same way.  Hence, it is perfectly normal to see svchost.exe loading a DLL from atypical locations.</p>

<p>Clearly there is no silver bullet detection for DLL hijacking or persistence through service DLLs.  However it does not mean we should give up.  We have to approach it from a couple different angles.  First, we can filter the events using heuristics learned from human analysts.  The <a href=https://github.com/veramine/Detections/wiki/Suspicious-Image-Loads#detecting-dll-side-loading-attacks>Veramine Detections wiki</a> describes the type of heuristics we are using for this rules-based algorithm.  In addition, we can apply machine learning models on the collected data (we will write more about this in the future).  Second, we assume that all current detection techniques by humans and machines will fail and then we have an opportunity to learn about the malware after the fact.  In this scenario, we need to be able to retroactively apply the new knowledge to all previous events.  At Veramine, we call this capability “time travel incident response.”  We structure the event data in a way such that they can be “replayed” in time so analysts can find something that may have been missed earlier, even years earlier.  You can think of it like a DVR for endpoint security events.  How is this useful?  Here are two examples...</p>

<P>First, suppose you read <a href=https://www.symantec.com/connect/blogs/longhorn-tools-used-cyberespionage-group-linked-vault-7>Symantec’s blog about the CIA malware</a> and learned that one of the backdoors used the service DLL as the primary persistence mechanism (“Graphics Utility Mgmt Service”).  Of course you want to know if this service ever existed on your network.  The only way to answer this question is to look at the service instrumentation logs.  The Veramine platform tracks all service creation and deletion events and conveniently stores them in a log file suitable for later replay.  These logs are compact and can be stored indefinitely, limited only by either our on premises customers disk space or our Windows Azure storage account.  Naturally, we also log all DLL load events so you can go back in time to investigate them or "replay" the information into a different security monitoring solution.</p>

<P>Next, lets say you are interested in investigating every instance of the Icon Overlay persistence creation as described in the Wikileaks articles.  The Veramine endpoint client sends most interesting registry writes to the Veramine server to enable detection, search, and response related to registry writes.  One such algorithm we added just today highlights the creation of new icon overlays and adds a detection.  Using the TortoiseSVN from the Wikileaks article, we see the following detection result added:</P>

<a href=img/e16-1.png><img width=1280 src=img/e16-1.png></a>

<p>The defender can click on the msiexec.exe link to see how the msiexec.exe (PID 5364) was lauched that created this new icon overlay:</P>

<a href=img/e16-2.png><img width=1280 src=img/e16-2.png></a>

<p>The same msiexec.exe process detail page also shows all the binaries loaded by this process.  In this case, here were the four most recent loaded modules:</p>

<a href=img/e16-3.png><img width=800 src=img/e16-3.png></a>

<p>Clicking on any of these binaries reveals this was likely to be a legitimate software install and not a CIA implant to spy on us.  For extra verification, one could click the Download link to get a copy of this binary as the Veramine platform saves a single copy of every unique binary loaded by any process across the corporate network.</P>

<a href=img/e16-4.png><img width=800 src=img/e16-4.png></a>

<p>Service operations, DLL loads, and registry writes are only a few of the events we collect and efficiently display, index, and store for long-term archival.  In the future we will discuss other events we collect and how they can be used in daily “threat hunting”, incident response, and forensic analysis operations.</p>

<p>As a final note, we would like to emphasize that we would love to have you use our product to perform this type of Collection, Analysis, and Detection but you can absolutely achieve similar results in a number of different ways with your own infrastructure.  If you already have deep visibility into low level system operations through a different deployed product or tool, we would be happy to highlight some of the things we have learned using our product that might help you make your collected data more actionable.  Feel free to hit us up on email (contact and/or jness and/or bruce at veramine.com) or our personal twitter handles below.</p>

